ns genuine calibration; the supervised-only model does not. Figure 1 shows one such item. Continuing from the same SFT adapter, verifier-grounded RL with a sparse outcome-only reward repairs and exceeds the base under both prompt styles (0.65 and 0.84 respectively). The dense reward also repairs the more demanding SymCE -prompt probe, but only to just above the base (0.34 against 0.27), where sparse reaches 0.65. The dense and sparse variants are statistically indistinguishable on the in-distribution counterexample benchmark of § 6.1 (Table 2 ) but differ by 31 percentage points on this probe on the reference seed, and by 33 ± \pm 8 points across four seeds. Appendix E shows the dissociation is larger still on the subset of true theorems whose mutations were never seen in training, so it is not a memorisation artefact. This dissociation, equal in-distribution Pass@1 with a large out-of-distribution gap, is interpretable as evidence that the two reward shapes induce qualitatively different policies despite producing the same counterexample-generation performance. We give the mechanism in § 6.3 . 6.3 Why it happens: a reason-taxonomy analysis Figure 2: Per-sample outcome distribution on the counterexample benchmark (greedy, n=201). The oracle assigns each rollout exactly one outcome: success ( q ¬ = 1 q_{
