eg}{=}0 , so the policy asserts the false conjecture is true), partial , parse error and no tag . The released records carry these as the codes success , neg_q_only , none_on_false , hyp_only , partial , oracle_parse_error and no_tag . We exploit the structured reward to label every test rollout with one of seven mutually exclusive outcomes (Figure 2 ). Three observations explain the imitation trap. SFT trades format errors for logical errors. The base model rarely emits the trained format at all: 55 % of its rollouts carry no tag. SFT solves this, but the corresponding mass does not move uniformly into success; instead, the hyp-only category, in which the policy satisfies the remaining hypotheses but asserts the false conclusion is true, rises from 1 % to 31 %. The supervised-only model learned the form of a counterexample without learning the calibration: it confidently emits objects that fail the negated-conclusion check. Sparse RL re-learns abstention. The abstention rate is 0 % under SFT, 2 % under dense RL, and 10 % under sparse RL. The sparse reward yields equal score for emitting a wrong witness and emitting NONE , so any rollout the policy is uncertain about is funnelled into abstention; under the dense reward, the partial-credit term α ⋅ ( s hyp / n hyp ) \alpha\cdot(s_{	ext{hyp}}/n_{	ext{hyp}}) makes “try and partially succeed” strictly better than “abstain”. This is the causal mechanism for the 31-percentage-point gap of Table 3 . Identical success rates hide diverging failure modes. While dense and sparse RL achieve nearly identical success fractions (0.502 vs 0.497), dense rewards push the remaining probability mass into over-eager hyp-only errors, whereas sparse rewards push it into abstention. We conclude that when a verifier yields a structured boolean tuple, an outcome-only aggregator preserves calibration better than partial credit. Rewarding satisfaction of some but not all hypotheses actively trains over-eager counterexample emission. The claim is specific to that term rather than to dense rewards in general, and our dense reward is not a strawman: its conclusion weighting ( β = 0.65 > α = 0.25  eta{=}0.65>\alpha{=}0.25 ) provably precludes the naive hypothesis-only dominance failure (§ 3.4 ), and it was selected after exploring several dense variants. 6.4 Cross-domain consistency Figure 3: Per-domain counterexample Pass@1 (greedy) with 95 % bootstrap CIs. Annotations on top: number of test items per domain. The largest absolute gain is on LinearAlgebra (+0.30); the most striking result is on RealAnalysis, where RLVR-sparse gains +0.23 absolute despite the domain being only 6.7 % of the RLVR training distribution. Figure 3 stratifies the main result by domain. Both RLVR variants improve on the base in every domain, with no sign of domain collapse or narrow specialisation. The largest relative gain is on Real Analysis (0.23 → 	o 0.46), despite it being the smallest slice of the RLVR training set at 6.7 %. This is the inverse of what an over-fitting model would do, and is consistent with the policy learning a transferable skill rather than a domain-conditional shortcut. Dense and sparse 95 % CIs overlap in almost every domain, supporting the in-distribution equivalence of § 6.1 per-domain rather than as an aggregation artefact. 6.5 Out-of-domain transfer Benchmark (n) base SFT dense sparse GSM8K (1319) 0.83 0.82 ± \pm .03 0.88 ± \pm .02 0.88 ± \pm .01 MATH-500 (500) 0.13 0.55 ± \pm .01 0.65 ± \pm .02 0.65 ± \pm .01 MMLU-CM (100) 0.34 0.54 ± \pm .03 0.70 ± \pm .05 0.72 ± \pm .04 AIME-2024 (30) 0.00 0.07 ± \pm .03 0.08 ± \pm .03 0.08 ± \pm .02 Table 4: Out-of-domain transfer under standard chain-of-thought prompting (greedy, Pass@1), mean ± \pm sd over all four training seeds. Identical prompt template across all four models; our SFT/RLVR pipeline contains no examples or answer-format training from these benchmarks. Table 4 measures transfer to four standard mathematical-reasoning benchmarks. Training improves on the base everywhere, with sparse and dense comparable. All four models are evaluated under identical chain-of-thought prompting, and our SFT and RLVR data contain no GSM8K, MATH, MMLU or AIME examples and no training on their answer-extraction formats, so the improvements are attributable to transfer rather than to format adaptation in the training pipeline. Part of the MATH-500 jump is nonetheless format adaptation: the base scores 0.13 largely because it does not follow the answer-extraction template, which SFT installs. GSM8K isolates the reasoning gain, because there the base already formats answers correctly (0.83), yet RLVR still reaches 0.88 under the identical prompt while SFT alone does not improve on the base at all (0.82 ± \pm 0.03). The transfer therefore arrives with the reinforcement stage, not the imitation stage. We interpret these results as supporting the broader narrative motivated in the introduction: counterexample-style supervision is a fundamental component of mathematical reasoning that conventional pre-training under-installs, rather than a narrow side-skill. 6.6 Robustness across seeds and model families Metric base SFT dense sparse Qwen3-4B, mean ± \pm sd over 4 seeds SymCE Pass@1 0.30 0.34 ± \pm .02 0.49 ± \pm .02 0.46 ± \pm .03 SymCE Pass@8 0.49 0.62 ± \pm .02 0.66 ± \pm .02 0.64 ± \pm .02 Truth SymCE -prompt 0.27 0.00 ± \pm .00 0.33 ± \pm .04 0.66 ± \pm .04 Truth-classify 0.36 0.16 ± \pm .05 0.71 ± \pm .09 0.75 ± \pm .13 Gemma-3-4B, seed 56 SymCE Pass@1 0.21 0.22 0.39 0.34 Truth-classify 0.49 0.01 0.53 0.59 Table 5: Robustness of the headline findings. SymCE Pass@ k k is the counterexample benchmark (n=201); the two Truth rows are the theorem-truth probe (n=347) under the two prompt styles of § 5.2 . Qwen3-4B seeds are 42, 19, 31 and 64; the base is training-seed independent. Bold marks the best value in a row. Table 5 repeats the pipeline with three further seeds and on a second model family; per-seed values are in Appendix D . The